l>\n<head>\n<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Fallback Video Title\">\n</head>\n<body>\n<script id='playerembed' src='https://static.example.com/player.js'></script>\n</body>\n</html>\n'''\n\nPLAYER_JS = u'''\n(function() {\n  $.ajax(url, opts);\n } })(12345, 0, [360, 720]);\n})();\n'''\n```\n\nWait \u2014 careful with the categories regex and the `><i` requirement. In WEBPAGE_FULL I have `<div>><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>`. The regex needs `><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>` \u2014 in `<div>><i ...`, we have `>` then `>` then `<i`. The regex `><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>` will match starting at the second `>`. Good.\n\nHmm, wait: `(?s)><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>\\s*Categories / Tags\\s*.*?<ul class=\"[^\"]*?list[^\"]*?\">(.*?)</ul>`. After `</i>` I have `\\nCategories / Tags\\n`. `\\s*` matches `\\n`. Then `Categories / Tags` matches. Then `\\s*` matches `\\n`. Then `.*?` (with DOTALL) matches `` (empty) up to `<ul class=\"list-tags\">`. Wait, `.*?<ul class=\"[^\"]*?list[^\"]*?\">` \u2014 non-greedy `.*?` then `<ul class=\"` then `[^\"]*?` (can be empty) then `list` \u2014 `list-tags` starts with `list`. Good. Then `\">(.*?)</ul>` captures the li's.\n\nThen findall `<li><a.*?>(.*?)</a>` on the captured html: `<li><a href=\"/tag/one\"> One </a>` \u2192 group ' One '; `<li><a href=\"/tag/two\">Two</a>` \u2192 'Two'. Stripped: ['One', 'Two']. \n\nNow the uploader regexes \u2014 my anchor line: `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"https://www.fourtube.com/channel/uploader1\" title=\"Go to Uploader One page\">Subscribe</a>`.\n\nRegex 1: `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"[^\"]+/(?:channel|user)s?/([^/\"]+)\" title=\"Go to [^\"]+ page\">`. \n\n- `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"` matches.\n- `[^\"]+` matches `https://www.fourtube.com` (greedy \u2014 could match more, but backtracking will find the right split). Actually `[^\"]+/(?:channel|user)s?/([^/\"]+)\"` \u2014 greedy `[^\"]+` will try to match as much as possible: `https://www.fourtube.com/channel/uploader1` then needs `/`... hmm. Let's see: the string inside href is `https://www.fourtube.com/channel/uploader1`. The regex after `href=\"` is `[^\"]+/(?:channel|user)s?/([^/\"]+)\"`. Greedy `[^\"]+` first tries to consume everything up to the closing `\"`: `https://www.fourtube.com/channel/uploader1` \u2014 then needs `/` but next char is `\"` \u2192 backtrack. Eventually `[^\"]+` = `https://www.fourtube.com`, then `/channel/` matches, then `([^/\"]+)` = `uploader1`, then `\"` matches. Then ` title=\"Go to [^\"]+ page\">` \u2014 ` title=\"Go to Uploader One page\">` \u2014 `[^\"]+` = `Uploader One`. Good. uploader_id = 'uploader1'.\n\nRegex 2: `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"[^\"]+/(?:channel|user)s?/[^/\"]+\" title=\"Go to ([^\"]+) page\">` \u2192 uploader = 'Uploader One'. Good.\n\nNow the meta for interactionCount: `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">`. Regex: `<meta[^>]+itemprop=\"interactionCount\"[^>]+content=\"UserPlays:([0-9,]+)\">`. `<meta` + `[^>]+` (space) + `itemprop=\"interactionCount\"` + `[^>]+` (space) + `content=\"UserPlays:1,234\"` \u2014 wait the regex is `content=\"UserPlays:([0-9,]+)\">`. So it needs `content=\"UserPlays:` then digits/commas then `\">`. My content is `UserPlays:1,234` \u2192 captures `1,234`, then `\">`. Good.\n\nBut careful: there are two interactionCount metas. `_search_regex` returns the first match. UserPlays meta comes first \u2192 view_count = str_to_int('1,234') = 1234. like_count regex searches for UserLikes \u2192 finds the second meta \u2192 567. Good.\n\nstr_to_int('1,234'): Let me recall the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef str_to_int(int_str):\n    \"\"\" A more flexible int string converter. See tests. \"\"\"\n    if int_str is None:\n        return None\n\n    int_str = re.sub(r'[,\\.]', '', int_str)\n    int_str = re.sub(r'^0+(.)', r'\\1', int_str)\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm no. Actual youtube_dl utils:\n\n```python\ndef str_to_int(int_str):\n    \"\"\" A more flexible int st