45, 0, [360, 720]`, then `\\)` matches `)`. \n\nThen `[%s]` % params_js \u2192 `[12345, 0, [360, 720]]` \u2192 json parse \u2192 [12345, 0, [360, 720]]. params[0] = 12345 (int!). media_id = 12345. sources = ['360', '720'].\n\nNote media_id is an int here (12345), not a string. That's how the real code behaves. So in the fallback test, `_extract_formats` receives media_id=12345 (int).\n\nHmm wait, `params[0]` \u2014 the JSON `[12345, 0, [360, 720]]` \u2192 params[0] = 12345. Yes int.\n\nSo in test 2, assert `_extract_formats` called with media_id == 12345 and sources == ['360', '720'].\n\nAlso in test 2, the `_download_webpage` fake must return the webpage on first call and player JS on second call. The second call is `self._download_webpage(player_js_url, video_id, 'Downloading player JS')`.\n\nTest 3: kind == 'm' branch (line 47-48). URL like `https://m.fourtube.com/videos/12345/some-title` \u2014 kind = 'm', so url is rewritten to `self._URL_TEMPLATE % video_id`. Then `_download_webpage` receives the rewritten URL. Assert the downloaded URL is the template URL.\n\nAlso `not display_id` branch: URL without display_id, e.g. `https://www.fourtube.com/videos/12345` \u2192 display_id is None \u2192 url rewritten. This covers the `not display_id` part of the condition. Actually line 47 `if kind == 'm' or not display_id:` \u2014 to cover both branches of the `or`, we need: (a) kind == 'm' with display_id present (short-circuit True), (b) kind != 'm' and display_id None (True via second), (c) kind != 'm' and display_id present (False \u2192 no rewrite). \n\nBranch coverage:\n- Test 1: kind='www' (or None) and display_id present \u2192 condition False \u2192 no rewrite. Covers the False branch.\n- Test 3a: kind='m', display_id present \u2192 True via first operand.\n- Test 3b: kind not 'm', no display_id \u2192 True via second operand.\n\nFor the real FourTubeIE _VALID_URL, I need to know the exact pattern. Let me query.\n\nAlso, what about `categories_html` being None (line 66-67 False branch)? In test 2's webpage, I can omit the categories section so `categories_html` is None \u2192 categories = None. That covers the False branch of `if categories_html:`. And test 1 covers the True branch. \n\nAlso missing meta tags: in test 2, I could omit some metas to test None handling (timestamp None, etc.). But careful: `title = self._html_search_meta('name', webpage)` \u2014 `_html_search_meta` default fatal=False, so returns None if missing. Then title=None. That's fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double check `_html_search_meta` signature in this version: `def _html_search_meta(self, name, html, display_name=None, fatal=False, **kwargs)`. Yes, fatal defaults to False. Good.\n\nNow, about `_search_regex` for player JS (line 88-90): `self._search_regex(r'...', webpage, 'player JS', group='url')` \u2014 no default, so fatal=True \u2192 raises if not found. In test 2, the webpage must contain the script tag.\n\nNow let me think about how `_download_webpage` is called: `self._download_webpage(url, video_id)` and `self._download_webpage(player_js_url, video_id, 'Downloading player JS')`. The real signature: `_download_webpage(self, url_or_request, video_id, note=None, errnote=None, fatal=True, ...)`. My fake: `def fake_download_webpage(self, url, video_id, *args, **kwargs)`. Since I'll monkeypatch on the instance with a plain function (not bound), I need to be careful: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)` where fake takes (url, video_id, ...) without self \u2014 because setting an instance attribute means it's called as `self._download_webpage(url, video_id)` \u2192 looks up instance attr \u2192 plain function \u2192 called with (url, video_id). Yes, instance attribute functions are not bound. So fake signature: `fake(url, video_id, note=None, **kwargs)`.\n\nAlternatively monkeypatch the class: `monkeypatch.setattr(FourTubeIE, '_download_webpage', fake)` where fake takes (self, url, ...). Either works. I'll patch instances to avoid class-level pollution concerns (monkeypatch undoes class-level too, but instance-level is cleaner).\n\nHmm, one concern: `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake)` \u2014 when undoing, monkeypatch will do `setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', <original bound method>)` because hasattr was True. That leaves an instance attribute pointing to the bound method \u2014 harmless since the instance is local to the test. Actually wait, does monkeypatch.setattr store the old value and restore it? Yes: it does `oldval = getattr(target, name)` (via `getattr`), then setattr new; on undo, if the attribute was present, setattr old back. So after the test, `ie._download_webpage` is an instance attribute = bound method. Instance discarded \u2192 no pollution. Fine.\n\nAlternatively, to be extra clean, I can create the IE inside the test and not worry.\n\nNow, which class to instantiate? The one with `_VALID_URL`. Let me check the module's classes. From youtube-dl's fourtube.py (around 2016-2021):\n\n```python\nclass FourTubeIE(FourTubeBaseIE):\n    IE_NAME = 'fourtube'\n    _VALID_URL = r'https?://(?:(?P<kind>www|m)\\.)?(?P<tld>fourtube|faptube)\\.com/(?:videos|embed)/(?P<id>\\d+)(?:/(?P<display_id>[^/?#]+))?'\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. There's also:\n\n```python\nclass FuxIE(FourTubeBaseIE):\n    _VALID_URL = r'https?://(?:www\\.)?fux\\.com/(?:video|embed)/(?P<id>\\d+)...'\nclass PornerBrosIE(FourTubeBaseIE):\n    _VALID_URL = r'https?://(?:www\\.)?pornerbros\\.com/videos/(?P<id>\\d+)...'\n```\n\nThe 'kind' group: In the real code, I believe FourTubeIE's _VALID_URL is:\n\n```python\n_VALID_URL = r'https?://(?:(?P<kind>www|m)\\.)?(?P<tld>fourtube|faptube)\\.com/(?:videos|embed)/(?P<id>\\d+)(?:/(?P<display_id>[^/?#]+))?'\n```\n\nHmm wait, but then `kind` would be 'www' or 'm'. And `if kind == 'm'` \u2192 mobile URL rewritten. And `not display_id` \u2192 rewrite. OK.\n\nLet me just query get_info for the actual classes. I'll query:\n- 'FourTubeBaseIE'\n- 'FourTubeIE'\n-