ame = [name]\n    if display_name is None:\n        display_name = name[0]\n\n    return self._html_search_regex(\n        r'''(?ix)<meta\n                    (?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?%s[\"\\']?)\n                    [^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']''' % regex_or(*name),\n        html, display_name, fatal=fatal, **kwargs)\n```\n\nHmm, versions differ. Older:\n\n```python\ndef _html_search_meta(self, name, html, display_name=None, fatal=False, **kwargs):\n    if not isinstance(name, (tuple, list)):\n        name = [name]\n    if display_name is None:\n        display_name = name[0]\n    return self._html_search_regex(\n        r'(?i)<meta[^>]+?name=[\"\\']%s[\"\\'][^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']' % '|'.join(map(re.escape, name)),\n        html, display_name, fatal=fatal, **kwargs)\n```\n\nHmm! Older versions only match `name=` attribute. Newer versions match itemprop/property too. This matters for the interactionCount metas \u2014 but those use `_search_regex` directly, not `_html_search_meta`. For `_html_search_meta('name', webpage)` \u2014 the meta has `name=\"name\"` \u2014 matches both variants. For 'uploadDate', 'thumbnailUrl', 'duration' \u2014 I'll use `name=` attributes to be safe across versions.\n\nWait, but there's a catch with the older regex: `<meta[^>]+?name=[\"\\']%s[\"\\'][^>]+?content=` \u2014 for `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">`: `<meta` then `[^>]+?` (lazy, at least 1 char) then `name=\"name\"` then `[^>]+?` then `content=\"...\"`. The first `[^>]+?` must match at least one char before `name=` \u2014 the space. Good. Then after `name=\"name\"`, `[^>]+?` matches ` ` then `content=\"Test Video Title\"`. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: with `name=\"name\"`, the regex `name=[\"\\']name[\"\\']` \u2014 the %s is 'name' \u2014 `name=[\"\\']name[\"\\']`. In `<meta name=\"name\" ...>`: matches. OK.\n\nBut hmm, what if the version's regex requires the meta tag to have name attribute and my `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" 