`not self._downloader.params.get('no_color')` first \u2192 AttributeError if _downloader is None.\n\nSo I MUST provide a downloader object with a `params` dict. Options:\n- Create a dummy object: `class _FakeDownloader: params = {}` and also `report_warning` maybe needed (in the else branch of _search_regex when fatal=False and no default... but we always pass default or the regex matches; for `_html_search_regex` with fatal=False and no default \u2014 wait: `uploader_id = self._html_search_regex(..., 'uploader id', fatal=False)` \u2014 no default passed! So if the regex doesn't match, `_search_regex` goes to `elif default is not NO_DEFAULT` \u2192 NO_DEFAULT \u2192 `elif fatal:` \u2192 False \u2192 `self._downloader.report_warning(...)`. So the downloader needs a `report_warning` method too!\n\nSo in the fallback test (no uploader anchor), `_html_search_regex(..., fatal=False)` will call `self._downloader.report_warning('unable to extract uploader id...')`. So my fake downloader needs `params` dict and `report_warning` method.\n\nAlso `_download_webpage` is patched, so no downloader usage there. `_extract_formats` patched.\n\nWhat else uses `self._downloader`? `_search_regex` (params, report_warning), `_html_search_meta` \u2192 `_html_search_regex` \u2192 `_search_regex`. `_parse_json` \u2014 no downloader. `clean_html` \u2014 no.\n\nAlso `bug_reports_message()` \u2014 fine.\n\nSo the fake downloader:\n\n```python\nclass _FakeDownloader(object):\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n        self.warnings = []\n\n    def report_warning(self, msg):\n        self.warnings.append(msg)\n```\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 also `sys.stderr.isatty()` \u2014 if `params.get('no_color')` is falsy and os is posix and stderr is a tty, then `_name` gets color codes \u2014 harmless. Under pytest, stderr is usually captured (not a tty) \u2014 but even if tty, harmless.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety: `if not self._downloader.params.get('no_color') and compat_os_name != 'nt' and sys.stderr.isatty():` \u2014 this is evaluated on EVERY `_search_regex` call. With my fake downloader having `params = {}`, `.get('no_color')` \u2192 None \u2192 `not None` \u2192 True \u2192 then `compat_os_name != 'nt'` \u2192 True on Linux \u2192 then `sys.stderr.isatty()` \u2192 False under pytest capture \u2192 `_name = name`. Fine. On a tty it would colorize the name \u2014 only affects error messages, not behavior. Fine.\n\nAlternatively set `params = {'no_color': True}` to short-circuit. I'll do that to be safe.\n\n6. `_html_search_meta` uses `self._meta_regex(n)` \u2014 let me check what `_meta_regex` produces:\n\n```python\ndef _meta_regex(self, prop):\n    '''Return a regex for parsing meta tags'''\n    return r'''(?is)<meta\n                    (?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?%s[\"\\']?)\n                    [^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']''' % re.escape(prop)\n```\n\nSomething like that. So `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"...\">` matches. Note `[\"\\']?` \u2014 quotes optional. And the lookahead requires itemprop|name|property = prop. Good.\n\nImportant: `_html_search_meta` passes `group='content'` to `_html_search_regex`. And `_html_search_regex` applies `clean_html(res).strip()` \u2014 so title 'Test Video Title' stays as-is. Good.\n\nWait \u2014 one important detail: `_html_search_regex` with `group='content'` \u2014 `_search_regex` with group='content' returns `mobj.group('content')`. The meta regex has group 'content'. Good.\n\n7. `parse_iso8601(date_str, delimiter='T', timezone=None)`:\n\n```python\ndef parse_iso8601(date_str, delimiter='T', timezone=None):\n    if date_str is None:\n        return None\n    date_str = re.sub('\\\\.[0-9]+', '', date_str)\n    if timezone is None:\n        timezone, date_str = extract_timezone(date_str)\n    try:\n        date_format = '%Y-%m-%d{0}%H:%M:%S'.format(delimiter)\n        dt = datetime.datetime.strptime(date_str, date_format) - timezone\n        return calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n```\n\nSo it calls `extract_timezone(date_str)` \u2014 which parses 'Z' or '+HH:MM' etc. Let me check extract_timezone:\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(r'^$from$ (?:Z|([+-])((?:[0-1][0-9]|2[0-3])(?::?([0-5][0-9]))?))', date_str)  # something\n```\n\nThe actual:\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(\n        r'(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?$',\n        date_str)\n    if not m:\n        tz = datetime.timedelta()\n    else:\n        sign = 1 if m.group('sign') == '+' else -1\n        hours = int(m.group('hours'))\n        minutes = int(m.group('minutes') or 0)\n        tz = sign * datetime.timedelta(hours=hours, minutes=minutes)  # hmm sign\n    if m:\n        date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group(0))]\n    return tz, date_str\n```\n\nHmm, what about 'Z'? I recall:\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(\n        r'(?:Z$|(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?$)',\n        date_str)\n    timezone = datetime.timedelta()\n    if m:\n        if not m.group('sign'):\n            date_str = date_str[:-1]  # strip Z\n        else:\n            sign = -1 if m.group('sign') == '-' else 1\n            hours = int(m.group('hours'))\n            minutes = int(m.group('minutes') or 0)\n            timezone = sign * datetime.timedelta(hours=hours, minutes=minutes)\n            date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group(0))]\n    return timezone, date_str\n```\n\nSomething like that. So '2016-05-07T12:34:56Z' \u2192 timezone = timedelta(0), date_str = '2016-05-07T12:34:56' \u2192 strptime OK \u2192 dt - timedelta(0) \u2192 timegm \u2192 1462624496.\n\nLet me double check my epoch computation: 2016-05-07 12:34:56 UTC.\n\nUsing calendar: days from 1970-01-01 to 2016-05-07.\nYears 1970..2015 complete = 46 years. Leap years in [1970, 2015]: 1972, 1976, 1980, 1984, 1988, 1992, 1996, 2000, 2004, 2008, 2012 \u2192 11. Days = 46*365 + 11 = 16790 + 11 = 16801 \u2192 2016-01-01 00:00 UTC = 16801 * 86400 = 1,462,579,200. Check: known value \u2014 2016-01-01 00:00:00 UTC = 1451606400. Hmm! 1451606400 / 86400 = 16801.0? 16801 * 86400 = 1,462,579,200. But known 1451606400... 1451606400 / 86400 = 16800.07...? Let me compute: 86400 * 16800 = 1,451,520,000. Hmm, 1451606400 - 1451520000 = 86400 \u2192 so 2016-01-01 = 16801 days \u2192 1,451,520,000 + 86,400 = 1,451,606,400. Yes! I made an arithmetic error before: 16801 * 86400: 16801 * 86400 = 16801 * 86,400. 16000*86400 = 1,382,400,000. 800*86400 = 69,120,000. 1*86400 = 86,400. Total = 1,382,400,000 + 69,120,000 + 86,400 = 1,451,606,400. Yes! I previously miscalculated (1,462,579,200 was wrong). So 2016-01-01 00:00 UTC = 1,451,606,400. \u2713 (matches known value 1451606400).\n\nNow days from 2016-01-01 to 2016-05-07: Jan (31) + Feb (29, 2016 leap) + Mar (31) + Apr (30) = 121 days to 2016-05-01. May 7 = 121 + 6 = 127 days after Jan 1. 127 * 86400 = 10,972,800. 1,451,606,400 + 10,972,800 = 1,462,579,200. Interesting \u2014 so 2016-05-07 00:00 UTC = 1,462,579,200. Then + 12:34:56 = 45,296 s \u2192 1,462,624,496.\n\nSo timestamp = 1462624496. My original number was right after all (the intermediate was wrong but final right). Let me double-check with another anchor: 2016-05-07... Known: 2016-06-01 00:00 UTC = 1464739200. 2016-05-01 = 1464739200 - 31*86400 = 1464739200 - 2678400 = 1462060800. Then 2016-05-07 = 1462060800 + 6*86400 = 1462060800 + 518400 = 1462579200. Hmm! That gives 1,462,579,200 for 2016-05-07 00:00. \u2713 consistent. Then + 45296 = 1,462,624,496. \u2713.\n\nSo expected timestamp = 1462624496. I'll compute it in the test via `calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))` to avoid hardcoding errors \u2014 calendar.timegm((2016,5,7,12,34,56,0,0,0)) = 1462624496. Good, and it's self-documenting.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 does `extract_timezone` handle 'Z'? If this version's extract_timezone doesn't handle 'Z', strptime would fail on '2016-05-07T12:34:56Z' (ValueError) \u2192 parse_iso8601 returns None. To be safe, I could use '+00:00' offset instead: '2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00' \u2014 the regex `[+-][0-9]{2}:?[0-9]{2}?$` handles that. Or use no timezone at all \u2014 then extract_timezone returns timedelta(0)? If no match... hmm, in some versions, if no tz found, timezone defaults to... Let me check extract_timezone via get_info to be certain. Let me query it.\n\nActually, let me query `extract_timezone` and also `set_downloader` and `_meta_regex` and `clean_html` (probably not needed). And `NO_DEFAULT`/`int_or_none` not needed.\n\n8. `parse_duration('330')`: first regex `(?:(?:(?:(?P<days>[0-9]+):)?(?P<hours>[0-9]+):)?(?P<mins>[0-9]+):)?(?P<secs>[0-9]+)(?P<ms>\\.[0-9]+)?Z?$` \u2014 '330' \u2192 secs='330' \u2192 duration = 330.0. So parse_duration('330') = 330.0 (float!). Note: it returns float 330.0. So `result['duration'] == 330` \u2192 True (330.0 == 330). Good. I'll assert `== 330`.\n\nHmm wait, actually the first regex on '330': `(?:(?:(?:(?P<days>[0-9]+):)?(?P<hours>[0-9]+):)?(?P<mins>[0-9]+):)?(?P<secs>[0-9]+)...` \u2014 the optional prefix groups don't participate, secs='330'. m matches. duration = float('330') = 330.0. \u2713.\n\n9. `str_to_int('1,234')`: `re.sub('[,\\\\.\\\\+]', '', '1,234')` \u2192 '1234' \u2192 `int_or_none('1234')` \u2192 1234. \u2713. And str_to_int(None) \u2192 falls through: not int, not str \u2192 returns None implicitly. \u2713.\n\n10. `_extract_formats` real implementation uses `self._TKN_HOST`, `compat_urlparse`, `_download_json`, `_sort_formats` \u2014 we patch it entirely, so no network. Good.\n\n11. `FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/%s/video'`. So rewritten URL for video 12345: 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'.\n\nNow the URLs for tests:\n- Test 1 (display_id present, kind='www'): 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title' \u2192 kind='www', id='12345', display_id='some-display-title'. Condition `kind == 'm' or not display_id` \u2192 False \u2192 url unchanged. downloaded == [url].\n- Test 2 (kind='m'): 'https://m.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title' \u2192 kind='m' \u2192 rewritten to 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'.\n- Test 3 (no display_id): 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345' \u2192 display_id None \u2192 rewritten.\n- Test 4 (fallback): 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/99999/fallback-title' \u2192 unchanged; two downloads.\n\nAlso embed URL: 'http://www.4tube.com/embed/209733' \u2014 matches _VALID_URL (videos|embed), display_id None \u2192 rewritten. Could use as test 3 variant. I'll use the plain videos URL.\n\nNow, the fake downloader needs `params` and `report_warning`. Let me check `set_downloader` to be sure `_downloader` is set to None when downloader=None. Let me query `InfoExtractor.set_downloader` and `extract_timezone` and `_meta_regex`.\n\nAlso \u2014 `bug_reports_message()` \u2014 from youtube_dl.utils \u2014 fine.\n\nOne more: `_search_regex` \u2014 `if not self._downloader.params.get('no_color')...` \u2014 with fake downloader params={'no_color': True} \u2192 `not True` \u2192 False \u2192 short-circuits, skips stderr check. \n\nAnd in the fatal=False-no-match path: `self._downloader.report_warning('unable to extract %s' % _name + bug_reports_message())` \u2014 needs report_warning. My fake has it.\n\nNow \u2014 `_html_search_meta` calls `_html_search_regex([self._meta_regex(n) for n in name], html, display_name, fatal=fatal, group='content')`. `_html_search_regex` \u2192 `_search_regex(pattern_list, ...)`. In `_search_regex`, pattern is a list \u2192 loops. OK.\n\n`_meta_regex` \u2014 let me check its exact regex to craft meta tags. Query it.\n\nLet me also double check `clean_html` on 'Test Video Title' \u2192 unchanged. On ' One ' \u2192 wait, categories don't go through clean_html (they use `re.findall` directly). uploader goes through `_html_search_regex` \u2192 clean_html('uploader1').strip() \u2192 'uploader1'. Good.\n\nNow, `title = self._html_search_meta('name', webpage)` \u2014 hmm, `_meta_regex('name')` \u2014 the lookahead `(?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?)`. For `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">`: `<meta` then lookahead at position after `<meta`: `[^>]+` = ` ` then `name=` then `[\"\\']?` = `\"` then `name` then `[\"\\']?` = `\"` \u2014 lookahead satisfied. Then `[^>]+?` lazy \u2014 needs to consume up to `content=`. `[^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']` \u2014 lazy `[^>]+?` matches ` name=\"name\" ` then `content=\"` then captures `Test Video Title` then `\"`. \n\nBut wait \u2014 could the regex match a DIFFERENT meta first? The regex is searched over the whole webpage. The first `<meta` tag in the document is `<meta name=\"name\" ...>`. For `_meta_regex('name')`, the first match: re.search scans from position 0. The first `<meta` occurrence is our title meta. Lookahead satisfied \u2192 match. Good.\n\nFor `_meta_regex('uploadDate')`: first `<meta` is the name meta \u2014 lookahead requires `itemprop|name|property=[\"\\']?uploadDate[\"\\']?` \u2014 `<meta name=\"name\"...` \u2014 after `<meta`, `[^>]+` could consume ` name=` then lookahead at `\"name\"` \u2014 needs `uploadDate` \u2014 no. `[^>]+` continues consuming... within the same tag (since `[^>]` can't cross `>`), positions: after `<meta`, try each split point. The name meta has no 'uploadDate' \u2192 no match there. re.search then tries the next `<meta` \u2014 the uploadDate meta \u2192 matches. Good.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: `re.search` finds the leftmost match. The pattern starts with `<meta`. For the name meta, can the pattern match starting at `<meta name=\"name\"...`? The lookahead must be satisfied somewhere within that tag. `[^>]+` can consume any amount of non-> chars, and the lookahead checks `(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?uploadDate[\"\\']?` at that point. In `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">`, is there a position where `name=\"uploadDate\"`... no. What about `content=\"Test Video Title\">` \u2014 no. So no match starting at the first `<meta`. Then the search moves on. Eventually matches the uploadDate meta. \u2713.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 one more subtlety: the pattern `(?is)<meta(?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?uploadDate[\"\\']?)[^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']` \u2014 the `[^>]+?content=` part: after the lookahead position (which is right before `name=\"uploadDate\"`), `[^>]+?` lazily consumes `name=\"uploadDate\"` etc. then `content=\"2016-05-07T12:34:56Z\"` \u2192 captures the datetime. \u2713.\n\nWait, I need to double check `_meta_regex` exact form. Let me query it. If it's the older form `r'(?i)<meta[^>]+?name=[\"\\']%s[\"\\'][^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']'` \u2014 hmm, but the get_info result showed `_html_search_meta` uses `self._meta_regex(n)` and group='content', which is the newer style. Let me query `_meta_regex`.\n\nAlso `extract_timezone` \u2014 query.\n\nAlso `set_downloader` \u2014 query.\n\nLet me also think: do I need `InfoExtractor._download_json`? No \u2014 `_extract_formats` is patched.\n\nWhat about `self._sort_formats`? Patched away with `_extract_formats`. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one more consideration: `FourTubeIE()` instantiation calls `InfoExtractor.__init__` \u2192 `set_downloader(None)` \u2192 `self._downloader = None`. Then I create the fake downloader and do `ie.set_downloader(fake)` \u2014 that's the cleanest way to inject it! `ie.set_downloader(_FakeDownloader())`. \n\nNow let me also double-check `_search_regex`'s use of `compat_os_name` and `sys.stderr.isatty()` \u2014 with no_color=True in params, short-circuited. \u2713.\n\nNow, the fallback test: `_search_regex(r'<script[^>]id=...', webpage, 'player JS', group='url')` \u2014 fatal=True (default), and the regex matches \u2192 returns url. \u2713.\n\n`params_js = self._search_regex(r'\\$\\.ajax\\(url,\\ opts\\);\\s*\\}\\s*\\}\\)\\(([0-9,\\[\\] ]+)\\)', player_js, 'initialization parameters')` \u2014 group=None \u2192 `next((g for g in mobj.groups() if g is not None))` \u2192 the first (only) group. \u2713.\n\n`params = self._parse_json('[%s]' % params_js, video_id)` \u2192 json.loads('[12345, 0, [360, 720]]') \u2192 [12345, 0, [360, 720]]. \u2713.\n\n`media_id = params[0]` \u2192 12345. `sources = ['%s' % p for p in params[2]]` \u2192 ['360', '720']. \u2713.\n\nThen `formats = self._extract_formats(url, video_id, media_id, sources)` \u2014 patched. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 PLAYER_JS content check against the regex once more:\n\n```\n$.ajax(url, opts);\n } })(12345, 0, [360, 720]);\n```\n\nRegex: `\\$\\.ajax\\(url,\\ opts\\);\\s*\\}\\s*\\}\\)\\(([0-9,\\[\\] ]+)\\)`\n\n- `\\$\\.ajax\\(url,\\ opts\\);` \u2192 `$.ajax(url, opts);` \u2713\n- `\\s*` \u2192 `\\n ` (newline + space before `}`) \u2713\n- `\\}` \u2192 `}` \u2713\n- `\\s*` \u2192 `\\n ` \u2713\n- `\\}\\)\\(` \u2192 `})(` \u2713\n- `([0-9,\\[\\] ]+)` \u2192 `12345, 0, [360, 720]` \u2713\n- `\\)` \u2192 `)` \u2713\n\nThe capture: `12345, 0, [360, 720]`. JSON: `[12345, 0, [360, 720]]` \u2713.\n\nNow the script tag regex on FALLBACK_WEBPAGE: `<script[^>]id=([\"\\'])playerembed\\1[^>]+src=([\"\\'])(?P<url>.+?)\\2`.\n\nMy tag: `<script id='playerembed' src='https://static.example.com/player.js'></script>`.\n\n- `<script` \u2713\n- `[^>]` \u2192 ` ` \u2713 (exactly one char)\n- `id=` \u2713\n- `([\"\\'])` \u2192 `'` \u2713\n- `playerembed` \u2713\n- `\\1` \u2192 `'` \u2713\n- `[^>]+` \u2192 ` ` \u2713\n- `src=` \u2713\n- `([\"\\'])` \u2192 `'` \u2713\n- `(?P<url>.+?)` \u2192 `https://static.example.com/player.js` \u2713\n- `\\2` \u2192 `'` \u2713\n\n\u2713.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but there's a catch: `re.search` scans the whole webpage. The FALLBACK_WEBPAGE contains `<script id='playerembed' ...>`. Also `</script>` \u2014 the pattern needs `<script` \u2014 `</script>` doesn't start with `<script` (it's `</s...`). \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 the FULL_WEBPAGE: I must double check the media_id regex against BOTH buttons and that sources picks up both qualities:\n\n`<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"360\">360p</button>` \u2014 media_id regex: `<button[^>]+data-id=([\"\\'])(?P<id>\\d+)\\1[^>]+data-quality=` \u2192 matches, id='12345'. \u2713\n\nsources findall: `<button[^>]+data-quality=([\"\\'])(.+?)\\1`:\n- Button 1: `<button ` then `[^>]+` \u2014 hmm wait: `<button[^>]+data-quality=` \u2014 `[^>]+` must match at least one char between `<button` and `data-quality=`. ` ` \u2713. Then `([\"\\'])` = `\"`, `(.+?)` = `360`, `\\1` = `\"`. \u2713\n- Button 2: `720`. \u2713\n\nsources = ['360', '720']. \u2713\n\nNow \u2014 categories regex on FULL_WEBPAGE. Let me write the exact HTML and trace:\n\n```\n<div>><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>\nCategories / Tags\n<ul class=\"list-tags\">\n<li><a href=\"/tag/one\"> One </a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/tag/two\">Two</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n```\n\nRegex: `(?s)><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>\\s*Categories / Tags\\s*.*?<ul class=\"[^\"]*?list[^\"]*?\">(.*?)</ul>`\n\n- `>` \u2014 the second `>` in `<div>>` \u2713 (first `>` closes div, second is literal text; regex finds the match starting at the second `>`... wait, actually re.search scans: at the first `>` (end of `<div>`), the next char is `>` not `<` \u2014 so `><i` fails there. At the second `>`, next is `<i class=...` \u2713).\n- `<i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>` \u2713\n- `\\s*` \u2192 `\\n` \u2713\n- `Categories / Tags` \u2713\n- `\\s*` \u2192 `\\n` \u2713\n- `.*?` \u2192 `` (empty, since `<ul` immediately follows) \u2713\n- `<ul class=\"` \u2713\n- `[^\"]*?` \u2192 `` \u2713\n- `list` \u2192 `list` \u2713\n- `[^\"]*?` \u2192 `-tags` \u2713\n- `\">` \u2713\n- `(.*?)` \u2192 `\\n<li><a href=\"/tag/one\"> One </a></li>\\n<li><a href=\"/tag/two\">Two</a></li>\\n` \u2713\n- `</ul>` \u2713\n\nfindall `(?s)<li><a.*?>(.*?)</a>`:\n- `<li><a href=\"/tag/one\">` \u2192 `.*?` = ` href=\"/tag/one\"`, group = ` One ` \u2713\n- `<li><a href=\"/tag/two\">` \u2192 group = `Two` \u2713\n\ncategories = ['One', 'Two'] \u2713\n\nUploader regexes on FULL_WEBPAGE:\n\n`<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"https://www.4tube.com/channel/wcp-club\" title=\"Go to WCP Club page\">Subscribe</a>`\n\n- uploader_id regex: `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"[^\"]+/(?:channel|user)s?/([^/\"]+)\" title=\"Go to [^\"]+ page\">` \u2192 `[^\"]+` = `https://www.4tube.com`, `/channel/`, `([^/\"]+)` = `wcp-club`, `\"`, ` title=\"Go to WCP Club page\">` \u2713 \u2192 'wcp-club'. Then clean_html \u2192 'wcp-club'. \u2713\n- uploader regex \u2192 `([^\"]+)` = `WCP Club` \u2192 clean_html('WCP Club').strip() \u2192 'WCP Club' \u2713\n\nI'll use 'wcp-club'/'WCP Club' to mirror the real test data. \n\nView/like counts:\n\n```\n<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">\n<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserLikes:567\">\n```\n\n- view regex: `<meta[^>]+itemprop=\"interactionCount\"[^>]+content=\"UserPlays:([0-9,]+)\">` \u2192 captures '1,234' \u2192 str_to_int \u2192 1234 \u2713\n- like regex \u2192 '567' \u2192 567 \u2713\n\nHmm wait \u2014 careful: the view regex `<meta[^>]+itemprop=\"interactionCount\"[^>]+content=\"UserPlays:...` \u2014 re.search scans; the first interactionCount meta is UserPlays \u2192 matches \u2713. The like regex skips the UserPlays meta (content doesn't match UserLikes) and matches the second \u2713.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtle issue: `[^>]+` between `itemprop=\"interactionCount\"` and `content=` \u2014 in my meta, there's just a space \u2713.\n\nDuration: `<meta name=\"duration\" content=\"330\">` \u2192 `_html_search_meta('duration', webpage)` \u2192 '330' \u2192 parse_duration('330') \u2192 330.0 \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, one concern with `_meta_regex('duration')`: the lookahead `(?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?duration[\"\\']?)`. My duration meta: `<meta name=\"duration\" content=\"330\">` \u2713.\n\nBut \u2014 could the duration meta regex accidentally match an earlier meta? Scanning: first `<meta name=\"name\"...` \u2014 lookahead needs `duration` \u2014 no. `<meta name=\"uploadDate\"...` \u2014 no. `<meta name=\"thumbnailUrl\"...` \u2014 no. `<meta name=\"duration\"...` \u2014 \u2713. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually wait: there's a potential false-match issue with `_meta_regex('name')` and the interactionCount metas: `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">` \u2014 lookahead needs `itemprop|name|property=[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?` \u2014 `itemprop=\"interactionCount\"` \u2014 after `<meta`, `[^>]+` consumes ` ` then lookahead at `itemprop=\"interactionCount\"` \u2014 needs `itemprop=\"name\"` \u2014 no. Continue: `[^>]+` consumes more... at position before `content=`: lookahead needs `(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?` \u2014 `content=\"UserPlays...\"` \u2014 no. So no match \u2713. Good.\n\nAnd `_meta_regex('name')` on `<meta name=\"uploadDate\" ...>`: lookahead needs `name=\"name\"` \u2014 the tag has `name=\"uploadDate\"` \u2014 at position before `name=`, lookahead checks `name=[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?` \u2192 `name=\"uploadDate\"` \u2014 `[\"\\']?` matches `\"`, then needs literal `name` but has `uploadDate` \u2014 no. Hmm wait \u2014 `[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?` \u2014 could it match `name=\"uploadDate\"` partially? The lookahead regex is `(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?` \u2014 at the position right before `name=\"uploadDate\"`, it tries `name=` \u2713, `[\"\\']?` \u2192 `\"`, then literal `name` vs `uploadDate` \u2717. Backtrack: `[\"\\']?` \u2192 empty, then `name` vs `uploadDate` \u2717. No match. Then `[^>]+` advances past `name=`... at position before `\"uploadDate\"`: lookahead needs `itemprop|name|property=` \u2014 `\"uploadDate\"` starts with `\"` \u2717. So no \u2713.\n\nOK. Now \u2014 the timestamp meta: `<meta name=\"uploadDate\" content=\"2016-05-07T12:34:56Z\">`. parse_iso8601('2016-05-07T12:34:56Z'):\n\n- `re.sub('\\\\.[0-9]+', '', date_str)` \u2014 no dots... wait, the string has no '.' \u2014 \u2713 unchanged.\n- `extract_timezone('2016-05-07T12:34:56Z')` \u2014 need to verify it handles 'Z'. Pending query.\n- strptime('2016-05-07T12:34:56', '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S') \u2713\n- dt - timezone(timedelta(0)) \u2192 timegm \u2192 1462624496.\n\nI'll verify extract_timezone. If it doesn't handle 'Z', I'll use '+00:00'.\n\nActually, let me look at the youtube-dl source for extract_timezone (from memory of utils.py circa 2016+):\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(\n        r'^$from$ (?:Z|(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?)$',\n        date_str)\n    timezone = datetime.timedelta()\n    if m:\n        if not m.group('sign'):\n            date_str = date_str[:-1]\n        else:\n            sign = -1 if m.group('sign') == '-' else 1\n            hours = int(m.group('hours'))\n            minutes = int(m.group('minutes') or 0)\n            timezone = sign * datetime.timedelta(\n                hours=hours, minutes=minutes)\n            date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group(0))]\n    return timezone, date_str\n```\n\nHmm, something like that \u2014 I believe 'Z' is handled (the regex includes `Z|`). Let me query to confirm.\n\nLet me also double check `int_or_none` \u2014 not needed directly.\n\nNow, about `set_downloader` \u2014 query to confirm it just assigns.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, one more thing: `InfoExtractor.__init__` sets `self._ready = False` and `self._x_forwarded_for_ip = None`. `_download_webpage` patched \u2192 doesn't touch those. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 is there any issue calling `ie._real_extract(url)` directly? No.\n\nLet me also think about whether to test the `embed` URL form (kind='www', no display_id): 'http://www.4tube.com/embed/209733' \u2014 matches `(?:videos|embed)`. I could use this for the no-display_id test to also exercise the embed alternative. The rewritten URL would be 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/209733/video'. Nice \u2014 covers the `embed` alternative in _VALID_URL too. But careful: the URL scheme http vs https \u2014 irrelevant since we patch download.\n\nHmm, but for the no-display_id test I want to reuse FULL_WEBPAGE (which has media buttons) so only one download happens. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 assertion for `_extract_formats` call args in test 1: `(url, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])` where url is the original URL (unchanged). \u2713.\n\nIn test 2 (m.): `_extract_formats` receives the REWRITTEN url ('https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'). \u2713 \u2014 assert that.\n\nIn test 3 (no display_id): rewritten url. \u2713.\n\nIn test 4 (fallback): original url (has display_id, kind='www') + media_id=12345 (int) + sources ['360','720']. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 the fake `_extract_formats` returns formats list; assert `result['formats']` equals it.\n\nLet me now write the fake downloader class:\n\n```python\nclass _FakeDownloader(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal downloader stand-in with the attributes InfoExtractor touches.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {'no_color': True}\n        self.warnings = []\n\n    def report_warning(self, message):\n        self.warnings.append(message)\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 does anything else call downloader methods? `_search_regex` fatal=False path \u2192 report_warning \u2713. Anything else in the executed code? `_parse_json` fatal path \u2192 raises ExtractorError, no downloader. `_html_search_meta` \u2192 `_html_search_regex` \u2192 `_search_regex`. \u2713.\n\nWait \u2014 also `bug_reports_message()`:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    if ytdl_is_updateable():  # no...\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    '''A message that is added to reported errors'''\n    return ytdl_bug... \n```\n\nHmm, in youtube_dl/utils.py:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    if ytdl_is_updateable():\n        update_command = 'ytdl ...'\n    ...\n```\n\nNo \u2014 the real one:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    '''A shortcut for adding bug reports message to warnings/errors'''\n    msg = '; please report this issue on https://github.com/ytdl-org/youtube-dl'\n    ...\n```\n\nIt's just a string function, no side effects. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 one more potential gotcha: `_search_regex` accesses `self._downloader.params.get('no_color')` BEFORE checking mobj. So even successful searches need the downloader. \u2713 we set it.\n\nNow, let me also confirm `set_downloader`:\n\n```python\ndef set_downloader(self, downloader):\n    \"\"\"Set the downloader for this IE.\"\"\"\n    self._downloader = downloader\n```\n\nQuery to confirm.\n\nNow \u2014 writing the final test file. Also need to handle: `from youtube_dl.extractor.fourtube import FourTubeBaseIE, FourTubeIE`.\n\nLet me also add a test that directly exercises... hmm, do I need a test for `categories_html` present but the `if categories_html:` False branch? Test 4 covers False (no categories section). Test 1 covers True. \u2713.\n\nBranch coverage of line 47 `if kind == 'm' or not display_id:`:\n- True via kind=='m': test 2 \u2713\n- True via not display_id: test 3 \u2713\n- False: test 1 & 4 \u2713\n\nLine 86 `if not (media_id and sources):`:\n- False (skip fallback): tests 1-3 \u2713\n- True (fallback): test 4 \u2713\n\nLine 67 `if categories_html:`:\n- True: tests 1-3 \u2713\n- False: test 4 \u2713\n\nAll lines 44-113 covered across the four tests. \n\nNow \u2014 one more check on the FULL_WEBPAGE: the `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">` \u2014 hmm, wait. `_meta_regex('name')` \u2014 the lookahead `(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?`. For the title meta `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">`: after `<meta`, `[^>]+` = ` `, lookahead at `name=\"name\"` \u2192 `name=` \u2713 `[\"\\']?` \u2192 `\"` `name` \u2713 `[\"\\']?` \u2192 `\"` \u2713. Then `[^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']` \u2014 lazy: `[^>]+?` = ` name=\"name\" ` hmm wait, after the lookahead (zero-width at position before `name=\"name\"`), the regex continues `[^>]+?content=[\"\\']...`. `[^>]+?` lazily expands: first tries 1 char `n`... then `content=` must match at `ame=\"name\" content=...` \u2014 no... expands until position before `content=\"Test Video Title\">` \u2014 `[^>]+?` = `name=\"name\" ` then `content=\"` \u2713 then `(.*?)` = `Test Video Title` then `[\"\\']` = `\"` \u2713. \n\nThen `_html_search_regex` \u2192 clean_html('Test Video Title').strip() \u2192 'Test Video Title' \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, actually there's a subtlety with `.*?` and `[\"\\']`: content 'Test Video Title' has no quotes \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 thumbnail meta: `<meta name=\"thumbnailUrl\" content=\"https://img.example.com/thumb.jpg\">` \u2192 'https://img.example.com/thumb.jpg' \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing I want to double-check: `_meta_regex` exact implementation. Let me query it, plus `extract_timezone`, plus `set_downloader`. Three more calls.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, `clean_html` \u2014 for 'WCP Club' \u2192 unchanged \u2713.\n\nLet me also consider: should the tests avoid depending on `FourTubeIE._TESTS`? Not used.\n\nOne more consideration \u2014 the module import: `from youtube_dl.extractor.fourtube import FourTubeBaseIE, FourTubeIE`. The extractor package `youtube_dl.extractor` has an `__init__` that imports all extractors (genextractorish) \u2014 importing `youtube_dl.extractor.fourtube` directly triggers `youtube_dl/extractor/__init__.py`? Yes, importing a submodule imports the package first, which imports ALL extractors (via lazy_extractors or the _all list). That's fine \u2014 it's just imports, no network. It might be slow-ish but OK.\n\nNow, let me write the final test:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Tests for youtube_dl.extractor.fourtube.FourTubeBaseIE._real_extract.\"\"\"\n\nimport calendar\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.fourtube import FourTubeBaseIE, FourTubeIE\n\n\nFULL_WEBPAGE = u'''\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">\n<meta name=\"uploadDate\" content=\"2016-05-07T12:34:56Z\">\n<meta name=\"thumbnailUrl\" content=\"https://img.example.com/thumb.jpg\">\n<meta name=\"duration\" content=\"330\">\n<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">\n<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserLikes:567\">\n</head>\n<body>\n<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"https://www.4tube.com/channel/wcp-club\" title=\"Go to WCP Club page\">Subscribe</a>\n<div>><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>\nCategories / Tags\n<ul class=\"list-tags\">\n<li><a href=\"/tag/one\"> One </a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/tag/two\">Two</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"360\">360p</button>\n<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"720\">720p</button>\n</body>\n</html>\n'''\n\nFALLBACK_WEBPAGE = u'''\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Fallback Video Title\">\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"player\">\n<script id='playerembed' src='https://static.example.com/player.js'></script>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n'''\n\nPLAYER_JS = u'''\n(function(global) {\n  function init() {\n    $.ajax(url, opts);\n  }\n })(12345, 0, [360, 720]);\n})();\n'''\n\nEXPECTED_TIMESTAMP = calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))\n\n\nclass FakeDownloader(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal downloader stub; InfoExtractor only touches params/report_warning.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {'no_color': True}\n        self.warnings = []\n\n    def report_warning(self, message):\n        self.warnings.append(message)\n\n\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, pages):\n    \"\"\"Build a FourTubeIE with network access stubbed out.\n\n    Returns (ie, downloaded_urls, format_calls); ``pages`` is the list of\n    HTML/JS bodies returned by successive _download_webpage calls.\n    \"\"\"\n    ie = FourTubeIE()\n    ie.set_downloader(FakeDownloader())\n\n    downloaded = []\n    format_calls = []\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(url, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        downloaded.append(url)\n        return pages.pop(0)\n\n    def fake_extract_formats(url, video_id, media_id, sources):\n        format_calls.append((url, video_id, media_id, list(sources)))\n        return [\n            {'format_id': quality + 'p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/%s.mp4' % quality}\n            for quality in sources]\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_formats', fake_extract_formats)\n    return ie, downloaded, format_calls\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_uses_webpage_buttons_when_present(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(monkeypatch, [FULL_WEBPAGE])\n\n    url = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    # The page was downloaded once, under the original URL (display_id present).\n    assert downloaded == [url]\n\n    assert result['id'] == '12345'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\n    assert result['timestamp'] == EXPECTED_TIMESTAMP\n    assert result['thumbnail'] == 'https://img.example.com/thumb.jpg'\n    assert result['uploader_id'] == 'wcp-club'\n    assert result['uploader'] == 'WCP Club'\n    assert result['categories'] == ['One', 'Two']\n    assert result['view_count'] == 1234\n    assert result['like_count'] == 567\n    assert result['duration'] == 330\n    assert result['age_limit'] == 18\n\n    assert result['formats'] == [\n        {'format_id': '360p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/360.mp4'},\n        {'format_id': '720p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/720.mp4'},\n    ]\n    assert format_calls == [(url, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_rewrites_mobile_url(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(monkeypatch, [FULL_WEBPAGE])\n\n    url = 'https://m.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    canonical = FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE % '12345'\n    assert canonical == 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'\n    assert downloaded == [canonical]\n    assert result['id'] == '12345'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\n    assert format_calls == [(canonical, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_rewrites_url_without_display_id(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(monkeypatch, [FULL_WEBPAGE])\n\n    url = 'http://www.4tube.com/embed/12345'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    canonical = FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE % '12345'\n    assert downloaded == [canonical]\n    assert result['id'] == '12345'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\n    assert format_calls == [(canonical, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_falls_back_to_player_js(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(\n        monkeypatch, [FALLBACK_WEBPAGE, PLAYER_JS])\n\n    url = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/99999/fallback-title'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    # Webpage first, then the player JS referenced by the playerembed script.\n    assert downloaded == [url, 'https://static.example.com/player.js']\n\n    assert result['id'] == '99999'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Fallback Video Title'\n    # Everything except the title is missing from the minimal page.\n    assert result['categories'] is None\n    assert result['thumbnail'] is None\n    assert result['uploader'] is None\n    assert result['uploader_id'] is None\n    assert result['timestamp'] is None\n    assert result['like_count'] is None\n    assert result['view_count'] is None\n    assert result['duration'] is None\n    assert result['age_limit'] == 18\n\n    # media id / sources were recovered from the player JS parameters.\n    assert format_calls == [(url, '99999', 12345, ['360', '720'])]\n    assert result['formats'] == [\n        {'format_id': '360p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/360.mp4'},\n        {'format_id': '720p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/720.mp4'},\n    ]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 in the fallback test, `_html_search_regex` for uploader with fatal=False and NO match \u2192 `_search_regex` \