   except ValueError:\n        pass\n```\n\nSo it calls `extract_timezone(date_str)` \u2014 which parses 'Z' or '+HH:MM' etc. Let me check extract_timezone:\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(r'^$from$ (?:Z|([+-])((?:[0-1][0-9]|2[0-3])(?::?([0-5][0-9]))?))', date_str)  # something\n```\n\nThe actual:\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(\n        r'(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?$',\n        date_str)\n    if not m:\n        tz = datetime.timedelta()\n    else:\n        sign = 1 if m.group('sign') == '+' else -1\n        hours = int(m.group('hours'))\n        minutes = int(m.group('minutes') or 0)\n        tz = sign * datetime.timedelta(hours=hours, minutes=minutes)  # hmm sign\n    if m:\n        date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group(0))]\n    return tz, date_str\n```\n\nHmm, what about 'Z'? I recall:\n\n```python\ndef extract_timezone(date_str):\n    m = re.search(\n        r'(?:Z$|(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?$)',\n        date_str)\n    timezone = datetime.timedelta()\n    if m:\n        if not m.group('sign'):\n            date_str = date_str[:-1]  # strip Z\n        else:\n            sign = -1 if m.group('sign') == '-' else 1\n            hours = int(m.group('hours'))\n            minutes = int(m.group('minutes') or 0)\n            timezone = sign * datetime.timedelta(hours=hours, minutes=minutes)\n            date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group(0))]\n    return timezone, date_str\n```\n\nSomething like that. So '2016-05-07T12:34:56Z' \u2192 timezone = timedelta(0), date_str = '2016-05-07T12:34:56' \u2192 strptime OK \u2192 dt - timedelta(0) \u2192 timegm \u2192 1462624496.\n\nLet me double check my epoch computation: 2016-05-07 12:34:56 UTC.\n\nUsing calendar: days from 1970-01-01 to 2016-05-07.\nYears 1970..2015 complete = 46 years. Leap years in [1970, 2015]: 1972, 1976, 1980, 1984, 1988, 1992, 1996, 2000, 2004, 2008, 2012 \u2192 11. Days = 46*365 + 11 = 16790 + 11 = 16801 \u2192 2016-01-01 00:00 UTC = 16801 * 86400 = 1,462,579,200. Check: known value \u2014 2016-01-01 00:00:00 UTC = 1451606400. Hmm! 1451606400 / 86400 = 16801.0? 16801 * 86400 = 1,462,579,200. But known 1451606400... 1451606400 / 86400 = 16800.07...? Let me compute: 86400 * 16800 = 1,451,520,000. Hmm, 1451606400 - 1451520000 = 86400 \u2192 so 2016-01-01 = 16801 days \u2192 1,451,520,000 + 86,400 = 1,451,606,400. Yes! I made an arithmetic error before: 16801 * 86400: 16801 * 86400 = 16801 * 86,400. 16000*86400 = 1,382,400,000. 800*86400 = 69,120,000. 1*86400 = 86,400. Total = 1,382,400,000 + 69,120,000 + 86,400 = 1,451,606,400. Yes! I previously miscalculated (1,462,579,200 was wrong). So 2016-01-01 00:00 UTC = 1,451,606,400. \u2713 (matches known value 1451606400).\n\nNow days from 2016-01-01 to 2016-05-07: Jan (31) + Feb (29, 2016 leap) + Mar (31) + Apr (30) = 121 days to 2016-05-01. May 7 = 121 + 6 = 127 days after Jan 1. 127 * 86400 = 10,972,800. 1,451,606,400 + 10,972,800 = 1,462,579,200. Interesting \u2014 so 2016-05-07 00:00 UTC = 1,462,579,200. Then + 12:34:56 = 45,296 s \u2192 1,462,624,496.\n\nSo timestamp = 1462624496. My original number was right after all (the intermediate was wrong but final right). Let me double-check with another anchor: 2016-05-07... Known: 2016-06-01 00:00 UTC = 1464739200. 2016-05-01 = 1464739200 - 31*86400 = 1464739200 - 2678400 = 1462060800. Then 2016-05-07 = 1462060800 + 6*86400 = 1462060800 + 518400 = 1462579200. Hmm! That gives 1,462,579,200 for 2016-05-07 00:00. \u2713 consistent. Then + 45296 = 1,462,624,496. \u2713.\n\nSo expected timestamp = 1462624496. I'll compute it in the test via `calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))` to avoid hardcoding errors \u2014 calendar.timegm((2016,5,7,12,34,56,0,0,0)) = 1462624496. Good, and it's self-documenting.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 does `extract_timezone` handle 'Z'? If this version's extract_timezone doesn't handle 'Z', strptime would fail on '2016-05-