isplay_id` \u2192 False \u2192 url unchanged. downloaded == [url].\n- Test 2 (kind='m'): 'https://m.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title' \u2192 kind='m' \u2192 rewritten to 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'.\n- Test 3 (no display_id): 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345' \u2192 display_id None \u2192 rewritten.\n- Test 4 (fallback): 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/99999/fallback-title' \u2192 unchanged; two downloads.\n\nAlso embed URL: 'http://www.4tube.com/embed/209733' \u2014 matches _VALID_URL (videos|embed), display_id None \u2192 rewritten. Could use as test 3 variant. I'll use the plain videos URL.\n\nNow, the fake downloader needs `params` and `report_warning`. Let me check `set_downloader` to be sure `_downloader` is set to None when downloader=None. Let me query `InfoExtractor.set_downloader` and `extract_timezone` and `_meta_regex`.\n\nAlso \u2014 `bug_reports_message()` \u2014 from youtube_dl.utils \u2014 fine.\n\nOne more: `_search_regex` \u2014 `if not self._downloader.params.get('no_color')...` \u2014 with fake downloader params={'no_color': True} \u2192 `not True` \u2192 False \u2192 short-circuits, skips stderr check. \n\nAnd in the fatal=False-no-match path: `self._downloader.report_warning('unable to extract %s' % _name + bug_reports_message())` \u2014 needs report_warning. My fake has it.\n\nNow \u2014 `_html_search_meta` calls `_html_search_regex([self._meta_regex(n) for n in name], html, display_name, fatal=fatal, group='content')`. `_html_search_regex` \u2192 `_search_regex(pattern_list, ...)`. In `_search_regex`, pattern is a list \u2192 loops. OK.\n\n`_meta_regex` \u2014 let me check its exact regex to craft meta tags. Query it.\n\nLet me also double check `clean_html` on 'Test Video Title' \u2192 unchanged. On ' One ' \u2192 wait, categories don't go through clean_html (they use `re.findall` directly). uploader goes through `_html_search_regex` \u2192 clean_html('uploader1').strip() \u2192 'uploader1'. Good.\n\nNow, `title = self._html_search_meta('name', webpage)` \u2014 hmm, `_meta_regex('name')` \u2014 the lookahead `(?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?name[\"\\']?)`. For `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">`: `<meta` then lookahead at position after `<meta`: `[^>]+` = ` ` then `name=` then `[\"\\']?` = `\"` then `name` then `[\"\\']?` = `\"` \u2014 lookahead satisfied. Then `[^>]+?` lazy \u2014 needs to consume up to `content=`. `[^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']` \u2014 lazy `[^>]+?` matches ` name=\"name\" ` then `content=\"` then captures `Test Video Title` then `\"`. \n\nBut wait \u2014 could the regex match a DIFFERENT meta first? The regex is searched over the whole webpage. The first `<meta` tag in the document is `<meta name=\"name\" ...>`. For `_meta_regex('name')`, the first match: re.search scans from position 0. The first `<meta` occurrence is our title meta. Lookahead satisfied \u2192 match. Good.\n\nFor `_meta_regex('uploadDate')`: first `<meta` is the name meta \u2014 lookahead requires `itemprop|name|property=[\"\\']?uploadDate[\"\\']?` \u2014 `<meta name=\"name\"...` \u2014 after `<meta`, `[^>]+` could consume ` name=` then lookahead at `\"name\"` \u2014 needs `uploadDate` \u2014 no. `[^>]+` continues consuming... within the same tag (since `[^>]` can't cross `>`), positions: after `<meta`, try each split point. The name meta has no 'uploadDate' \u2192 no match there. re.search then tries the next `<meta` \u2014 the uploadDate meta \u2192 matches. Good.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: `re.search` finds the leftmost match. The pattern starts with `<meta`. For the name meta, can the pattern match starting at `<meta name=\"name\"...`? The lookahead must be satisfied somewhere within that tag. `[^>]+` can consume any amount of non-> chars, and the lookahead checks `(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?uploadDate[\"\\']?` at that point. In `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">`, is there a position where `name=\"uploadDate\"`... no. What about `content=\"Test Video Title\">` \u2014 no. So no match starting at the first `<meta`. Then the search moves on. Eventually matches the uploadDate meta. \u2713.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 one more subtlety: the pattern `(?is)<meta(?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property)=[\"\\']?uploadDate[\"\\']?)[^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']` \u2014 the `[^>]+?content=` part: after the lookahead position (which is right before `name=\"uploadDate\"`), `[^>]+?` lazily consumes `name=\"uploadDate\"` etc. then `content=\"2016-05-07T12:34:56Z\"` \u2192 captures the datetime. \u2713.\n\nWait, I need to double check `_meta_regex` exact form. Let me query it. If it's the older form `r'(?i)<meta[^>]+?name=[\"\\']%s[\"\\'][^>]+?content=[\"\\'](.*?)[\"\\']'` \u2014 hmm, but the get_info result showed `_html_search_meta` uses `self._meta_regex(n)` and group='content', which is the newer style. Let me query `_meta_regex`.\n\nAlso `extract_timezone` \u2014 query.\n\nAlso `set_downloader` \u2014 query.\n\nLet me also think: do I need `InfoExtractor._download_json`? No \u2014 `_extract_formats` is patched.\n\nWhat about `self._sort_formats`? Patched away with `_extract_formats`. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one more consideration: `FourTubeIE()` instantiation calls `InfoExtractor.__init__` \u2192 `set_downloader(None)` \u2192 `self._downloader = None`. Then I create the fake downloader and do `ie.set_downloader(fake)` \u2014 that's the cleanest way to inject it! `ie.set_downloader(_FakeDownloader())`. \n\nNow let me also double-check `_search_regex`'s use of `compat_os_name` and `sys.stderr.isatty()` \u2014 with no_color=True in params, short-circuited. \u2713.\n\nNow, the fallback test: `_search_regex(r'<script[^>]id=...', webpage, 'player JS', group='url')` \u2014 fatal=True (default), and the regex matches \u2192 returns url. \u2713.\n\n`params_js = self._search_regex(r'\\$\\.ajax\\(url,\\ opts\\);\\s*\\}\\s*\\}\\)\\(([0-9,\\[\\] ]+)\\)', player_js, 'initialization parameters')` \u2014 group=None \u219