        "reasoning_content": "Let me finalize the test based on what I know.\n\nKey facts gathered:\n\n1. `FourTubeIE._VALID_URL = 'https?://(?:(?P<kind>www|m)\\\\.)?4tube\\\\.com/(?:videos|embed)/(?P<id>\\\\d+)(?:/(?P<display_id>[^/?#&]+))?'`\n2. `FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/%s/video'`\n3. `FourTubeIE._TKN_HOST = 'token.4tube.com'`\n4. `InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None)` \u2192 `set_downloader(downloader)` \u2192 `self._downloader = downloader`\n5. `_search_regex` accesses `self._downloader.params.get('no_color')` on every call, and calls `self._downloader.report_warning(...)` in the fatal=False-no-match path. So a fake downloader with `params` dict and `report_warning` is required.\n6. `_html_search_meta` uses `_meta_regex(prop)`:\n   `'(?isx)<meta\\n (?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property|id|http-equiv)=([\"\\\\\\']?)%s\\\\1)\\n [^>]+?content=([\"\\\\\\'])(?P<content>.*?)\\\\2'`\n   \n   Important: the lookahead requires the attribute value to be quoted-or-not with backreference `\\1` \u2014 `([\"\\']?)%s\\1`. If the quote is optional and captured, `\\1` must match the same. For `name=\"name\"`: `[\"\\']?` captures `\"`, then `name`, then `\\1` = `\"` \u2713.\n   \n   Also note: the attribute list includes `id` and `http-equiv` \u2014 so `<meta name=\"duration\" ...>` etc. all fine.\n   \n   Also note the content group requires a quote: `content=([\"\\'])(?P<content>.*?)\\2` \u2014 content must be quoted. My metas use double quotes \u2713.\n   \n   Also `(?isx)` \u2014 verbose mode! Whitespace in the pattern is ignored... wait, `(?x)` means free-spacing \u2014 the pattern has literal newlines/spaces which are ignored in verbose mode. But careful: in verbose mode, `#` starts a comment \u2014 my HTML has no `#` issue since that's the pattern, not the subject. The pattern itself contains no `#`. \u2713\n   \n   One subtlety with `(?x)` and the pattern: `content=([\"\\'])` \u2014 fine.\n   \n   Another subtlety: since the lookahead includes `id=` as an attribute alternative \u2014 hmm, `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" ...>` \u2014 for `_meta_regex('name')`, lookahead alternatives: itemprop|name|property|id|http-equiv = \"name\". In the interactionCount meta, is there any position where `id=[\"\\']?name\\1`? Hmm \u2014 `itemprop=\"interactionCount\"` \u2014 no `id=`. Wait... actually, hmm: could `[^>]+` position such that `id=` matches somewhere? The tag is `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">`. There's no `id=` substring... wait, actually there IS no \"id=\" in that string. OK.\n   \n   But WAIT \u2014 a critical one: for `_meta_regex('name')` on `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">` \u2014 fine.\n   \n   Hmm, but here's a trickier one: the lookahead `(?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property|id|http-equiv)=([\"\\']?)name\\1)` \u2014 for the meta `<meta name=\"uploadDate\" content=\"...\">`: is there a position where `id=[\"\\']?name\\1` matches? The tag content: ` name=\"uploadDate\" content=\"2016-05-07T12:34:56Z\"`. Looking for `id=` \u2014 no \"id=\" substring. \u2713\n   \n   And for `_meta_regex('duration')` on `<meta name=\"duration\" content=\"330\">`: lookahead needs `name=\"duration\"` \u2713 (or `id=\"duration\"` \u2014 no). \u2713\n   \n   And `_meta_regex('name')` must NOT match `<meta name=\"duration\" ...>`: lookahead needs value exactly `name` \u2014 `name=\"duration\"` \u2717; any `id=`? no. \u2713\n\n   Hmm wait, one more: `_meta_regex('name')` vs `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">` \u2014 alternatives include `id` \u2014 is there `id=` anywhere? \"itemprop\" contains no \"id=\". \u2713\n\n   And crucially \u2014 `_meta_regex('name')` vs the FIRST meta in my FULL_WEBPAGE. The first meta IS `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">` \u2713.\n\n   Hmm, wait \u2014 actually there's one more potential gotcha: `[^>]+?content=([\"\\'])(?P<content>.*?)\\2` \u2014 after the lookahead position (zero-width, right before `name=\"name\"`), `[^>]+?` lazily consumes `name=\"name\" ` then `content=\"` then captures `Test Video Title` then `\"`. \u2713\n\n7. `parse_iso8601(date_str, delimiter='T', timezone=None)`:\n   ```python\n   if date_str is None: return None\n   date_str = re.sub('\\\\.[0-9]+', '', date_str)\n   if timezone is None:\n       timezone, date_str = extract_timezone(date_str)\n   try:\n       date_format = '%Y-%m-%d{0}%H:%M:%S'.format(delimiter)\n       dt = datetime.datetime.strptime(date_str, date_format) - timezone\n       return calendar.timegm(dt.timetuple())\n   except ValueError:\n       pass\n   ```\n   \n   `extract_timezone` info was unavailable. Risk: does it handle 'Z'? In youtube-dl's utils.py, extract_timezone is:\n   ```python\n   def extract_timezone(date_str):\n       m = re.search(\n           r'(?:Z$|(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?$)',\n           date_str)\n       timezone = datetime.timedelta()\n       if m:\n           if not m.group('sign'):\n               date_str = date_str[:-1]\n           else:\n               sign = -1 if m.group('sign') == '-' else 1\n               hours = int(m.group('hours'))\n               minutes = int(m.group('minutes') or 0)\n               timezone = sign * datetime.timedelta(hours=hours, minutes=minutes)\n               date_str = date_str[:-len(m.group(0))]\n       return timezone, date_str\n   ```\n   I'm fairly confident 'Z' is handled (this is the standard youtube-dl implementation, and the fourtube extractor itself relies on parse_iso8601 for uploadDate values that end in Z or offsets). But since I couldn't verify, the safest approach for the timestamp assertion is to compare against `parse_iso8601('2016-05-07T12:34:56Z')` computed in the test itself, PLUS assert it's not None... but if parse_iso8601 returns None for 'Z', then `result['timestamp'] == parse_iso8601(...)` \u2192 None == None passes trivially, weakening the test but not breaking it.\n   \n   Better: use a '+00:00' offset format which extract_timezone definitely handles (the sign/hours/minutes branch is the core of that function): `2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00`. Then:\n   - extract_timezone: m matches `+00:00` \u2192 sign '+', hours '00', minutes '00' \u2192 timezone = timedelta(0), date_str = '2016-05-07T12:34:56'\n   - strptime \u2713 \u2192 timegm \u2192 1462624496.\n   \n   Hmm, but wait \u2014 the regex `(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?$` \u2014 for '+00:00': sign='+', hours='00', then ':?' matches ':', minutes='00' \u2713. date_str truncated by len('+00:00')=6 \u2192 '2016-05-07T12:34:56' \u2713.\n   \n   Actually, even safer: avoid the timezone question entirely by using a naive datetime string '2016-05-07T12:34:56' \u2014 then extract_timezone finds no match \u2192 timezone = timedelta(0) (in the implementation above, `timezone = datetime.timedelta()` is initialized before the `if m:` block, so no match \u2192 zero offset) \u2192 timegm treats it as UTC \u2192 1462624496. That works in the implementation I recalled. But if the actual extract_timezone raises or behaves differently on no-match... The standard implementation returns timedelta() for no match. Hmm.\n   \n   To be maximally robust, I'll assert:\n   ```python\n   assert result['timestamp'] == parse_iso8601('2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00')\n   assert result['timestamp'] is not None\n   ```\n   Wait \u2014 but if parse_iso8601 itself fails on that format, both would be None and the second assert fails, alerting me. Actually if parse_iso8601 can't parse it, the extractor would also produce None and the first assert passes while second fails \u2014 meaning my chosen format is bad. Using '+00:00' is the most standard ISO 8601 form; youtube-dl's parse_iso8601 tests include '2014-12-31T23:59:59+01:00' style values, so it's definitely supported. And I'll also hardcode the expected epoch 1462624496 as the primary assertion since I'm confident: parse_iso8601('2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00') = calendar.timegm(2016-05-07 12:34:56) - 0 = 1462624496.\n   \n   Let me double check 1462624496 once more: 2016-01-01 00:00 UTC = 1451606400 (well-known). Jan 31 + Feb 29 + Mar 31 + Apr 30 = 121 days \u2192 May 1 = 1451606400 + 121*86400 = 1451606400 + 10454400 = 1462060800. May 7 = +6*86400 = +518400 \u2192 1462579200. +12:34:56 = +45296 \u2192 1462624496. \u2713\n   \n   I'll compute it in the test with `calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))` \u2014 self-documenting and correct \u2014 and assert equality. That avoids hardcode-arithmetic risk while still pinning the exact value.\n\n8. `parse_duration('330')` \u2192 first regex matches secs='330' \u2192 330.0. Assert `== 330`. \u2713\n\n9. `str_to_int('1,234')` \u2192 re.sub removes ',' \u2192 '1234' \u2192 int_or_none \u2192 1234. \u2713 `str_to_int(None)` \u2192 not int, not str \u2192 returns None (implicit). \u2713\n\n10. `_extract_formats` real impl uses `self._TKN_HOST` and `_download_json` \u2014 we patch it, so no network. \u2713\n\n11. `clean_html` applied by `_html_search_regex` \u2014 'wcp-club' and 'WCP Club' unchanged. \u2713\n\nNow the four tests:\n\nTest A \u2014 full webpage, display_id present, kind='www': URL 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title'. No rewrite. All fields populated from webpage buttons. Covers lines 44-85 (except 87-97), 99, 101-113.\n\nTest B \u2014 kind='m': URL 'https://m.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title' \u2192 rewritten to 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'. Covers the `kind == 'm'` short-circuit branch of line 47-48.\n\nTest C \u2014 no display_id: URL 'http://www.4tube.com/embed/12345' \u2192 display_id None \u2192 rewritten. Covers the `not display_id` branch and the `embed` alternative of _VALID_URL.\n\nTest D \u2014 fallback: webpage without buttons but with playerembed script; player JS with `$.ajax(url, opts); } })(12345, 0, [360, 720]);`. Covers lines 86-97. Also covers `if categories_html:` False branch and all the None fallbacks (uploader/uploader_id via fatal=False \u2192 report_warning on fake downloader).\n\nScript tag regex: `<script[^>]id=([\"\\'])playerembed\\1[^>]+src=([\"\\'])(?P<url>.+?)\\2` \u2014 needs exactly one non-'>' char between '<script' and 'id='. So `<script id='playerembed' src='...'>` with a single space. \u2713\n\nPlayer JS regex: `\\$\\.ajax\\(url,\\ opts\\);\\s*\\}\\s*\\}\\)\\(([0-9,\\[\\] ]+)\\)` \u2014 my PLAYER_JS:\n```\n$.ajax(url, opts);\n } })(12345, 0, [360, 720]);\n```\n\u2713 params_js = '12345, 0, [360, 720]' \u2192 '[12345, 0, [360, 720]]' \u2192 params[0]=12345 (int), params[2]=[360, 720] \u2192 sources=['360','720'].\n\nCategories regex needs `><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>` \u2014 I'll put `<div>><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>` in the HTML.\n\nUploader anchor: `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"https://www.4tube.com/channel/wcp-club\" title=\"Go to WCP Club page\">Subscribe</a>`.\n\nButtons: `<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"360\">360p</button>` and `<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"720\">720p</button>`.\n\nMeta tags: name, uploadDate, thumbnailUrl, duration, and the two interactionCount metas.\n\nFake downloader: params={'no_color': True} (short-circuits the isatty check), report_warning collects messages.\n\nCleanup: all stubbing via monkeypatch on a locally-created IE instance; no global state modified; no network. The pages list is consumed via pop(0) so unexpected extra downloads raise IndexError (fail loudly).\n\nOne more check on `_meta_regex` and my `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">`: the lookahead is `(?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property|id|http-equiv)=([\"\\']?)name\\1)`. Position after `<meta`: `[^>]+` = ' ', then `name=` \u2713, `([\"\\']?)` captures '\"', `name` \u2713, `\\1` = '\"' \u2713. Then `[^>]+?content=([\"\\'])(?P<content>.*?)\\2` \u2192 captures 'Test Video Title'. \u2713\n\nAnd for uploadDate meta with content '2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00' \u2014 captured fully (no quotes inside). \u2713\n\nWait, one more check on `_meta_regex('name')` against `<meta name=\"uploadDate\" content=\"2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00\">`: lookahead alternatives \u2014 could `id=` match? No 'id=' substring. Could `name=\"name\"` match? `name=\"uploadDate\"` \u2014 `([\"\\']?)` captures '\"', then literal 'name' vs 'uploadDate' \u2717. \u2713 No match.\n\nAnd `_meta_regex('uploadDate')` must not match earlier metas \u2014 none contain 'uploadDate' as an attribute value. \u2713\n\nAlso `_meta_regex('thumbnailUrl')` and `_meta_regex('duration')` similar. \u2713\n\nNow, `interactionCount` regexes (plain `_search_regex`, not meta regex):\n- `<meta[^>]+itemprop=\"interactionCount\"[^>]+content=\"UserPlays:([0-9,]+)\">` vs `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">` \u2713 \u2192 '1,234' \u2192 1234\n- UserLikes:567 \u2192 567 \u2713\n\nMedia id regex: `<button[^>]+data-id=([\"\\'])(?P<id>\\d+)\\1[^>]+data-quality=` vs `<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"360\">` \u2713 \u2192 '12345'.\n\nSources findall: `<button[^>]+data-quality=([\"\\'])(.+?)\\1` \u2192 [('\",\"360'), ('\"','720')] \u2192 ['360','720'] \u2713.\n\nNow write the final file. Structure:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Tests covering FourTubeBaseIE._real_extract (youtube_dl/extractor/fourtube.py).\"\"\"\n\nimport calendar\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.fourtube import FourTubeBaseIE, FourTubeIE\n\nFULL_WEBPAGE = ...\nFALLBACK_WEBPAGE = ...\nPLAYER_JS = ...\n\nEXPECTED_TIMESTAMP = calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))\n\nEXPECTED_FORMATS = [\n    {'format_id': '360p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/360.mp4'},\n    {'format_id': '720p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/720.mp4'},\n]\n\n\nclass FakeDownloader(object):\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {'no_color': True}\n        self.warnings = []\n\n    def report_warning(self, message):\n        self.warnings.append(message)\n\n\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, pages):\n    ie = FourTubeIE()\n    ie.set_downloader(FakeDownloader())\n    downloaded = []\n    format_calls = []\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(url, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        downloaded.append(url)\n        return pages.pop(0)\n\n    def fake_extract_formats(url, video_id, media_id, sources):\n        format_calls.append((url, video_id, media_id, list(sources)))\n        return [dict(f) for f in EXPECTED_FORMATS]  # hmm, simpler: return copy\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_formats', fake_extract_formats)\n    return ie, downloaded, format_calls\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 for formats, simpler: fake returns a fresh list built from sources:\n\n```python\nreturn [{'format_id': q + 'p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/%s.mp4' % q} for q in sources]\n```\n\nThen EXPECTED_FORMATS asserted equal. Fine.\n\npytest import: I use the `monkeypatch` fixture \u2014 need `import pytest`? Actually monkeypatch is a fixture injected by pytest; I don't strictly need to import pytest unless using pytest.raises etc. But importing pytest is harmless and conventional. Actually, flake8 would flag unused import... I'll keep `import pytest` only if used. I don't use pytest.raises. I'll drop the import to keep it clean \u2014 but the fixture name `monkeypatch` doesn't require the import. Hmm, but some linters... it's fine. Actually, I'll keep it minimal without importing pytest. Wait \u2014 the instructions say \"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\" \u2014 monkeypatch fixture needs no import. OK, no pytest import needed. But to be safe and explicit, I'll import pytest anyway? No \u2014 unused import. I'll skip it. Hmm, actually many test files import pytest for no reason. I'll omit it since nothing from pytest is referenced.\n\nWait \u2014 actually, I should double-check: does anything raise ExtractorError that I should catch? No \u2014 all paths succeed.\n\nOne more consideration: `ie.set_downloader(FakeDownloader())` \u2014 is `set_downloader` the right injection point? Yes, confirmed signature.\n\nAlso \u2014 `FourTubeIE()` constructor: `InfoExtractor.__init__(self, downloader=None)` \u2192 fine.\n\nNow \u2014 assertion details for test A:\n\n```python\nurl = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title'\nresult = ie._real_extract(url)\n\nassert downloaded == [url]\nassert result['id'] == '12345'\nassert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\nassert result['timestamp'] == EXPECTED_TIMESTAMP\nassert result['thumbnail'] == 'https://img.example.com/thumb.jpg'\nassert result['uploader_id'] == 'wcp-club'\nassert result['uploader'] == 'WCP Club'\nassert result['categories'] == ['One', 'Two']\nassert result['view_count'] == 1234\nassert result['like_count'] == 567\nassert result['duration'] == 330\nassert result['age_limit'] == 18\nassert result['formats'] == EXPECTED_FORMATS\nassert format_calls == [(url, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n```\n\nTest B (mobile):\n\n```python\nurl = 'https://m.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title'\ncanonical = FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE % '12345'\nassert canonical == 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'\nresult = ie._real_extract(url)\nassert downloaded == [canonical]\nassert result['id'] == '12345'\nassert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\nassert format_calls == [(canonical, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n```\n\nTest C (embed / no display_id):\n\n```python\nurl = 'http://www.4tube.com/embed/12345'\ncanonical = FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE % '12345'\nresult = ie._real_extract(url)\nassert downloaded == [canonical]\nassert result['id'] == '12345'\nassert format_calls == [(canonical, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n```\n\nTest D (fallback):\n\n```python\nurl = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/99999/fallback-title'\nresult = ie._real_extract(url)\nassert downloaded == [url, 'https://static.example.com/player.js']\nassert result['id'] == '99999'\nassert result['title'] == 'Fallback Video Title'\nassert result['categories'] is None\nassert result['thumbnail'] is None\nassert result['uploader'] is None\nassert result['uploader_id'] is None\nassert result['timestamp'] is None\nassert result['like_count'] is None\nassert result['view_count'] is None\nassert result['duration'] is None\nassert result['age_limit'] == 18\nassert format_calls == [(url, '99999', 12345, ['360', '720'])]\nassert result['formats'] == EXPECTED_FORMATS\n```\n\nNote: media_id 12345 is an int in the fallback path (params[0] from JSON) \u2014 asserting that pins actual behavior.\n\nAlso in test D, the fake downloader accumulates warnings from the fatal=False searches (uploader, uploader_id, categories, uploadDate, thumbnailUrl, duration). I could assert `ie._downloader.warnings` non-empty as a bonus postcondition \u2014 shows the fatal=False paths executed. Let me add: `assert len(downloader.warnings) >= 5` \u2014 hmm, count: uploader id, uploader, categories, uploadDate meta, thumbnailUrl meta, duration meta \u2192 6 warnings. But relying on exact count is brittle; I'll assert that warnings were emitted (non-empty) and include 'uploader' in one of them. Actually the warning message format: `'unable to extract %s' % _name + bug_reports_message()` \u2014 for uploader id, name is 'uploader id'. So `any('uploader id' in w for w in warnings)`. I'll add a soft assertion: `assert downloader.warnings` \u2014 need access to the downloader. Let me have `_make_ie` return the downloader too, or attach it. I'll return it.\n\nHmm, keep it simple: `_make_ie` returns `(ie, downloaded, format_calls)` and I can access `ie._downloader.warnings`. That's fine.\n\nActually \u2014 wait, one potential problem in test D: `_html_search_meta('duration', webpage)` \u2192 `_html_search_regex(..., fatal=False, group='content')` \u2192 `_search_regex` \u2192 no match \u2192 fatal=False \u2192 report_warning \u2192 returns None \u2192 parse_duration(None) \u2192 None \u2713.\n\nAnd `_html_search_meta('uploadDate', ...)` \u2192 None \u2192 parse_iso8601(None) \u2192 None \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 one more careful check of the FALLBACK_WEBPAGE against `_meta_regex('name')`: `<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Fallback Video Title\">` \u2713.\n\nAnd the script regex needs `id=` preceded by exactly one non-'>' char after `<script`. My line: `<script id='playerembed' src='https://static.example.com/player.js'></script>` \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, also need to double check: in FALLBACK_WEBPAGE there are no `<button` tags \u2192 media_id None, sources [] \u2192 fallback \u2713. And no interactionCount metas \u2192 view/like None via default=None \u2713 (no warning for those, they use default).\n\nNow \u2014 FULL_WEBPAGE: I should double-check the `><i` construct doesn't break anything else. `<div>><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>` \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, one thing: in FULL_WEBPAGE, the categories `<ul class=\"list-tags\">` \u2014 regex `[^\"]*?list[^\"]*?` \u2713.\n\nLet me also double check the uploader regex against my anchor once more, char by char:\n\nAnchor: `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"https://www.4tube.com/channel/wcp-club\" title=\"Go to WCP Club page\">Subscribe</a>`\n\nRegex 1: `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"[^\"]+/(?:channel|user)s?/([^/\"]+)\" title=\"Go to [^\"]+ page\">`\n- `<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"` \u2713\n- `[^\"]+` = 'https://www.4tube.com' (after backtracking)\n- `/channel/` \u2713 (channel, s? \u2192 no s)\n- `([^/\"]+)` = 'wcp-club'\n- `\"` \u2713\n- ` title=\"Go to ` \u2713\n- `[^\"]+` = 'WCP Club'\n- ` page\">` \u2713\n\u2192 'wcp-club' \u2192 clean_html \u2192 'wcp-club' \u2713\n\nRegex 2: same but `[^/\"]+` non-capturing and `([^\"]+)` captures 'WCP Club' \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 Python 2/3: I'll use `u'''...'''` prefixes? In py3, u'' is fine (valid since 3.3). The youtube_dl codebase is py2-compatible but tests run under the repo's pytest (py3 almost surely). I'll use plain triple-quoted strings without u prefix \u2014 fine either way. Actually the regexes in the module operate on str; fine.\n\n`from __future__ import unicode_literals` \u2014 unnecessary in py3; skip.\n\nNow \u2014 one more thought about `monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)`: on teardown, monkeypatch restores by setattr'ing the original bound method onto the instance \u2014 leaves an instance attribute on a throwaway object. No global pollution. \u2713\n\nAlternatively I could patch the class \u2014 but instance patching avoids cross-test interference entirely. Keep instance patching.\n\nEdge: `pages.pop(0)` \u2014 if `_real_extract` (unexpectedly) downloads more than provided \u2192 IndexError \u2192 test fails. Good.\n\nAlso, should I assert that pages were fully consumed? `downloaded == [...]` already pins the count. \u2713\n\nOne more possible pitfall: `FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE % '12345'` \u2014 template is 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/%s/video' \u2192 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video' \u2713. I'll hardcode the expected canonical URL in assertions (stronger) \u2014 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'.\n\nNow, about the timestamp: use `calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))`. And the meta content: '2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00'. \n\nWait \u2014 one concern with '+00:00' and extract_timezone: the regex I recalled is `r'(?:Z$|(?P<sign>[+-])(?P<hours>[0-9]{2}):?(?P<minutes>[0-9]{2})?$)'`. For '+00:00': matches sign='+', hours='00', ':', minutes='00' \u2192 timezone = +timedelta(0) = 0 \u2192 date_str truncated \u2192 strptime OK \u2192 1462624496 \u2713.\n\nBut what if this version's extract_timezone differs (couldn't retrieve it)? The safest bet that works across ALL plausible implementations: '+00:00' is handled by any implementation that supports numeric offsets (which parse_iso8601's very existence implies \u2014 the fourtube real-world pages use offsets). 'Z' support is slightly less certain in old versions. So '+00:00' it is.\n\nHmm, actually wait \u2014 let me reconsider: what if extract_timezone's regex requires the colon (`[+-]\\d{2}:\\d{2}`) \u2014 '+00:00' has the colon \u2713. What if it requires no-colon? Unlikely. '+00:00' is the safest.\n\nAdditionally, to make the timestamp assertion robust even if my epoch arithmetic were wrong, I compute EXPECTED_TIMESTAMP via calendar.timegm \u2014 which is exactly what parse_iso8601 does for UTC. \u2713\n\nLet me also double-check `calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))` \u2014 timegm takes a 9-tuple; weekday/yday fields are ignored. \u2713 = 1462624496.\n\nNow, finalize the HTML strings. One more scan of FULL_WEBPAGE for accidental regex interference:\n\n- The `duration` meta: `<meta name=\"duration\" content=\"330\">`. `_meta_regex('duration')` lookahead: `name=\"duration\"` \u2713.\n- Hmm wait \u2014 DANGER: `_meta_regex('duration')` \u2014 the lookahead alternatives include `id`! Is there `id=\"duration\"` anywhere? No. \u2713\n- And `_meta_regex('name')` \u2014 could it match the thumbnailUrl meta via... no. \u2713\n\nAnother subtle one: `_meta_regex('thumbnailUrl')` \u2014 `re.escape('thumbnailUrl')` \u2192 'thumbnailUrl' \u2014 case-sensitive (only `(?is)` \u2014 i flag IS set! `(?isx)` includes `i` \u2014 case-insensitive!). So 'thumbnailUrl' matches case-insensitively \u2014 fine, my meta is exactly 'thumbnailUrl'. And 'uploadDate' \u2014 fine. No accidental cross-matches since values differ.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, case-insensitivity: `_meta_regex('name')` with `i` flag \u2014 `name` matches 'Name', 'NAME' etc. \u2014 my HTML uses lowercase \u2713. And the attribute lookup `name=` \u2014 case-insensitive \u2014 fine.\n\nOne more: with `(?i)`, `itemprop=\"interactionCount\"` \u2014 irrelevant for meta regex.\n\nOK. Also \u2014 the `(?x)` flag in _meta_regex: the PATTERN is verbose; my HTML is the SUBJECT \u2014 unaffected. \u2713\n\nNow \u2014 is there any chance `_meta_regex('name')` matches across the `<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">` because of `[^>]+?` crossing... no, `[^>]` can't cross `>`. \u2713\n\nAlright \u2014 also the title meta comes FIRST in head, so `re.search` for 'name' finds it first anyway.\n\nLet me also think again about whether `_html_search_meta('name', webpage)` could return something weird due to `(?s)` + lazy \u2014 no.\n\nFinal check on the fallback player JS regex group extraction: `_search_regex(pattern, player_js, 'initialization parameters')` \u2014 group=None \u2192 `next((g for g in mobj.groups() if g is not None))` \u2014 the pattern has ONE group \u2192 returns '12345, 0, [360, 720]' \u2713.\n\nAnd `'[12345, 0, [360, 720]]'` \u2192 json.loads \u2713 \u2192 [12345, 0, [360, 720]].\n\n`media_id = params[0]` \u2192 12345. `sources = ['%s' % p for p in params[2]]` \u2192 ['360', '720'] \u2713.\n\n`_extract_formats(url, video_id, 12345, ['360', '720'])` \u2014 patched \u2713.\n\nNow write the final file. I'll also add a docstring and keep top-level code minimal (only constants, class, helper function, four test functions \u2014 no pytest.main).\n\nAlso: the instruction says \"Respond ONLY with the Python code enclosed in backticks\" \u2014 the final answer must be just the code block.\n\nLet me also double check the `FakeDownloader.params` \u2014 `_search_regex` does `self._downloader.params.get('no_color')` \u2192 True \u2192 `not True` \u2192 False \u2192 skips isatty check \u2192 `_name = name` \u2713. No AttributeError.\n\nAnd `report_warning` \u2014 used in test D \u2713.\n\nOne more: `bug_reports_message()` \u2014 from youtube_dl.utils \u2014 pure function returning a string \u2713 (it may reference `ytdl_is_updateable`... actually in youtube_dl/utils.py:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    '''\n    A shortcut for adding a bug report message to a string.\n    ...\n    '''\n    return ytdl_bug_reports_message...\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    if ytdl_is_updateable():\n        ...\n```\n\nNo \u2014 the real one:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    '''A message that is appended to bugs/errors'''\n    return '; please report this issue on https://github.com/ytdl-org/youtube-dl ...'\n```\n\nIt's just a string. \u2713 No side effects.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually in some versions:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    if ytdl_is_updateable():\n```\n\nNo, that's yt-dlp. youtube_dl's is:\n\n```python\ndef bug_reports_message():\n    '''\n    A shortcut for adding a bug report message to a string.\n    '''\n    return '; please report this issue on https://github.com/ytdl-org/youtube-dl .'\n```\n\nSomething like that \u2014 pure. \u2713\n\nOK, writing the final answer now.\n\nActually \u2014 one more consideration on test D's warning assertion. The fatal=False searches that miss:\n1. uploader id (`_html_search_regex`, fatal=False, no default) \u2192 warning\n2. uploader \u2192 warning\n3. categories (`_search_regex`, fatal=False, no default) \u2192 warning\n4. uploadDate meta \u2192 warning (via _html_search_meta fatal=False)\n5. thumbnailUrl meta \u2192 warning\n6. duration meta \u2192 warning\n\nview/like use default=None \u2192 no warning. media id uses default=None \u2192 no warning.\n\nSo warnings should have 6 entries. I'll assert `len(ie._downloader.warnings) == 6`? That's brittle if my analysis is off. Safer: assert that warnings mention 'uploader id' and 'categories' \u2014 proving the fatal=False paths ran through the fake downloader rather than crashing. I'll do:\n\n```python\nwarnings = ' '.join(ie._downloader.warnings)\nassert 'uploader id' in warnings\nassert 'categories' in warnings\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 the warning text: `'unable to extract %s' % _name + bug_reports_message()` \u2192 \"unable to extract uploader id; please report...\" \u2713 contains 'uploader id'. And \"unable to extract categories...\" \u2713.\n\nWait \u2014 for the meta searches, display_name = name[0] \u2192 'uploadDate' etc. Fine, I won't assert those.\n\nActually hold on \u2014 is `_name` the colored name? With no_color=True, `_name = name` \u2713.\n\nGood. Also in tests A-C, no warnings should be needed (everything matches) \u2014 could assert `ie._downloader.warnings == []` in test A as a cleanliness check. Hmm \u2014 in test A, all fatal=False searches match (uploader, uploader_id, categories, all metas present) \u2192 no warnings \u2713. I'll add `assert ie._downloader.warnings == []` to test A. That's a nice postcondition. Actually wait \u2014 is everything really present in FULL_WEBPAGE? title \u2713, uploadDate \u2713, thumbnailUrl \u2713, uploader \u2713, uploader_id \u2713, categories \u2713, view \u2713, like \u2713, duration \u2713, media_id \u2713, sources \u2713. Yes \u2192 zero warnings \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 final structure. Also add `# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-`? Not needed; ASCII only. Skip.\n\nLet me write it.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Coverage tests for FourTubeBaseIE._real_extract (youtube_dl/extractor/fourtube.py).\n\nAll network access is stubbed out; each test builds a FourTubeIE instance\nlocally and restores everything via the ``monkeypatch`` fixture, so no global\nstate is polluted.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport calendar\n\nfrom youtube_dl.extractor.fourtube import FourTubeBaseIE, FourTubeIE\n\nFULL_WEBPAGE = \"\"\"\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Test Video Title\">\n<meta name=\"uploadDate\" content=\"2016-05-07T12:34:56+00:00\">\n<meta name=\"thumbnailUrl\" content=\"https://img.example.com/thumb.jpg\">\n<meta name=\"duration\" content=\"330\">\n<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserPlays:1,234\">\n<meta itemprop=\"interactionCount\" content=\"UserLikes:567\">\n</head>\n<body>\n<a class=\"item-to-subscribe\" href=\"https://www.4tube.com/channel/wcp-club\" title=\"Go to WCP Club page\">Subscribe</a>\n<div>><i class=\"icon icon-tag\"></i>\nCategories / Tags\n<ul class=\"list-tags\">\n<li><a href=\"/tag/one\"> One </a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/tag/two\">Two</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"360\">360p</button>\n<button data-id=\"12345\" data-quality=\"720\">720p</button>\n</body>\n</html>\n\"\"\"\n\nFALLBACK_WEBPAGE = \"\"\"\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta name=\"name\" content=\"Fallback Video Title\">\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"player\">\n<script id='playerembed' src='https://static.example.com/player.js'></script>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n\"\"\"\n\nPLAYER_JS = \"\"\"\n(function(global) {\n  function init() {\n    $.ajax(url, opts);\n  }\n })(12345, 0, [360, 720]);\n})();\n\"\"\"\n\n# 2016-05-07 12:34:56 UTC\nEXPECTED_TIMESTAMP = calendar.timegm((2016, 5, 7, 12, 34, 56, 0, 0, 0))\n\nEXPECTED_FORMATS = [\n    {'format_id': '360p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/360.mp4'},\n    {'format_id': '720p', 'url': 'https://fake.example.com/720.mp4'},\n]\n\n\nclass FakeDownloader(object):\n    \"\"\"Stand-in for the downloader: only `params` and `report_warning` are used.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {'no_color': True}\n        self.warnings = []\n\n    def report_warning(self, message):\n        self.warnings.append(message)\n\n\ndef _make_ie(monkeypatch, pages):\n    \"\"\"Create a FourTubeIE whose network access is replaced by `pages`.\"\"\"\n    ie = FourTubeIE()\n    downloader = FakeDownloader()\n    ie.set_downloader(downloader)\n\n    downloaded = []\n    format_calls = []\n\n    def fake_download_webpage(url, video_id, *args, **kwargs):\n        downloaded.append(url)\n        return pages.pop(0)\n\n    def fake_extract_formats(url, video_id, media_id, sources):\n        format_calls.append((url, video_id, media_id, list(sources)))\n        return [\n            {'format_id': quality + 'p',\n             'url': 'https://fake.example.com/%s.mp4' % quality}\n            for quality in sources]\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_download_webpage', fake_download_webpage)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ie, '_extract_formats', fake_extract_formats)\n    return ie, downloader, downloaded, format_calls\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_reads_media_data_from_page_buttons(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloader, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(monkeypatch, [FULL_WEBPAGE])\n\n    url = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    # display_id present and kind != 'm': the page URL is used unchanged.\n    assert downloaded == [url]\n    assert downloader.warnings == []\n\n    assert result['id'] == '12345'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\n    assert result['timestamp'] == EXPECTED_TIMESTAMP\n    assert result['thumbnail'] == 'https://img.example.com/thumb.jpg'\n    assert result['uploader_id'] == 'wcp-club'\n    assert result['uploader'] == 'WCP Club'\n    assert result['categories'] == ['One', 'Two']\n    assert result['view_count'] == 1234\n    assert result['like_count'] == 567\n    assert result['duration'] == 330\n    assert result['age_limit'] == 18\n    assert result['formats'] == EXPECTED_FORMATS\n\n    assert format_calls == [(url, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_rewrites_mobile_url_to_template(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloader, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(monkeypatch, [FULL_WEBPAGE])\n\n    url = 'https://m.4tube.com/videos/12345/some-display-title'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    canonical = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'\n    assert FourTubeIE._URL_TEMPLATE % '12345' == canonical\n    assert downloaded == [canonical]\n    assert result['id'] == '12345'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\n    assert result['age_limit'] == 18\n    assert format_calls == [(canonical, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_rewrites_url_without_display_id(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloader, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(monkeypatch, [FULL_WEBPAGE])\n\n    url = 'http://www.4tube.com/embed/12345'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    canonical = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/12345/video'\n    assert downloaded == [canonical]\n    assert result['id'] == '12345'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Test Video Title'\n    assert format_calls == [(canonical, '12345', '12345', ['360', '720'])]\n\n\ndef test_real_extract_falls_back_to_player_js(monkeypatch):\n    ie, downloader, downloaded, format_calls = _make_ie(\n        monkeypatch, [FALLBACK_WEBPAGE, PLAYER_JS])\n\n    url = 'https://www.4tube.com/videos/99999/fallback-title'\n    result = ie._real_extract(url)\n\n    # First the page, then the player JS referenced by the playerembed script.\n    assert downloaded == [url, 'https://static.example.com/player.js']\n\n    assert result['id'] == '99999'\n    assert result['title'] == 'Fallback Video Title'\n    assert result['categories'] is None\n    assert result['thumbnail'] is None\n    assert result['uploader'] is None\n    assert result['uploader_id'] is None\n    assert result['timestamp'] is None\n    assert result['like_count'] is None\n    assert result['view_count'] is None\n    assert result['duration'] is None\n    assert result['age_limit'] == 18\n    assert result['formats'] == EXPECTED_FORMATS\n\n    # media id/sources were recovered from the player JS; media id stays an int.\n    assert format_calls == [(url, '99999', 12345, ['360', '720'])]\n\n    # The non-fatal searches reported their failures through the downloader.\n    warnings = ' '.join(downloader.warnings)\n    assert 'uploader id' in warnings\n    assert 'categories' in warnings\n```\n\nWait \u2014 one issue in test D: `assert 'uploader id' in warnings` \u2014 the warning message is `'unable to extract %s' % _name + bug_reports_message()`. `_name` for the uploader id search is 'uploader id' \u2713. For categories: 'categories' \u2713.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 actually let me re-check: in `_search_regex`, the fatal=False branch: `self._downloader.report_warning('unable to extract %s' % _name + bug_reports_message())`. \u2713.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 is `_name` computed before the mobj check? Yes:\n\n```python\nif not self._downloader.params.get('no_color') and ...:\n    _name = colored\nelse:\n    _name = name\nif mobj: ...\n```\n\n\u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 one more re-examination of a potential problem in test A: `assert downloader.warnings == []`. Are ALL fatal=False searches satisfied in FULL_WEBPAGE? \n- uploader_id \u2713, uploader \u2713, categories \u2713, uploadDate \u2713, thumbnailUrl \u2713, duration \u2713. \n- view/like/media id use default=None (no warnings even on success/failure \u2014 they succeed anyway). \n\u2713 zero warnings.\n\nNow \u2014 another potential issue: in test A, `_html_search_meta('duration', webpage)` \u2192 '330' \u2192 parse_duration('330') \u2192 330.0. `result['duration'] == 330` \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, actually let me re-trace parse_duration('330') with the retrieved implementation:\n\n```python\ndef parse_duration(s):\n    if not isinstance(s, compat_basestring):\n        return None\n    s = s.strip()\n    days, hours, mins, secs, ms = [None] * 5\n    m = re.match('(?:(?:(?:(?P<days>[0-9]+):)?(?P<hours>[0-9]+):)?(?P<mins>[0-9]+):)?(?P<secs>[0-9]+)(?P<ms>\\\\.[0-9]+)?Z?$', s)\n    if m:\n        days, hours, mins, secs, ms = m.groups()\n    ...\n    duration = 0\n    if secs: duration += float(secs)\n    ...\n    return duration\n```\n\n'330' \u2192 secs='330' \u2192 330.0 \u2713.\n\nAnd parse_duration(None) \u2192 not a string \u2192 None \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 `str_to_int('1,234')`:\n```python\ndef str_to_int(int_str):\n    if isinstance(int_str, compat_integer_types): return int_str\n    elif isinstance(int_str, compat_str):\n        int_str = re.sub('[,\\\\.\\\\+]', '', int_str)\n        return int_or_none(int_str)\n```\n'1,234' \u2192 '1234' \u2192 int_or_none('1234') \u2192 1234 \u2713. str_to_int(None) \u2192 neither \u2192 returns None (implicit) \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 one more careful look at the `_meta_regex` pattern with `(?x)` verbose flag:\n\n```\n'(?isx)<meta\n                    (?=[^>]+(?:itemprop|name|property|id|http-equiv)=([\"\\\\\\']?)%s\\\\1)\n                    [^>]+?conte